# Abstract and title

The title and the abstract are pasted into the arXiv form and the journal's submission system as plain text. Both systems reject or mangle anything that needs the preamble.

## Length (hard requirement)

The abstract of `paper/main.tex` must be **1920 characters or fewer** (`abstract_limit` in `paper.toml`). This is an arXiv hard limit, not a preference. Do not finish an abstract edit that exceeds it.

- Count the collapsed abstract body (whitespace squeezed to single spaces), **including TeX commands**. For A&A's structured abstract, count all five parts together.
- Journals add word limits (often 250 words); the journal skill's reference file gives the one for the selected class.
- If the abstract is over the limit, cut whole side points (a list of methods, a second example, survey counts) before tightening sentences. `readability.mdc` rule 7 applies: do not compress.

## No custom macros

Forbidden in `\title{...}` and the abstract: every `\newcommand`, `\providecommand` and `\def` from the preamble and `paper/macros.tex` (`\Msun`, `\todo`, `\dd`, `\R`, the paper's own macros), and every citation command (`\citep`, `\citet`, `\cite`, ...). Standard TeX is fine: `\textit`, `\emph`, `$\approx$`, `$\sim$`, `$\beta$`, `$M_\odot$`, `~`.

```tex
% BAD
masses of 1.4\,\Msun{} \citep{Hogg2010}
% GOOD
masses of 1.4 $M_\odot$ (Hogg et al. 2010)
```

## Citations

Cite earlier work as plain text: "Hogg et al. (2010)". The abstract must not open with a citation command. It may open with the name of a proposal that the paper tests, if that proposal is its subject.

## Check

After any change to the title or abstract, run

```bash
python util/check_abstract.py
```

It prints the collapsed length, any forbidden macros in the abstract or the title, and whether the abstract opens with a citation command. It exits nonzero on a violation.
